@*
    One data table, in a scrollable region of its own.

    `.ms-main` hides horizontal overflow, and that is right: the shell must never grow a page-wide
    scrollbar because one table has twelve columns. What was wrong is that `.ms-table` is `width: 100%`
    with no scroll container, so a table wider than the content column was simply *cut off* - no
    scrollbar, no affordance, and no way at all to reach the columns on the right. The event-types table
    measured 1691px inside a 1200px column; four of its six columns were unreachable.

    So every data table goes in one of these. Three things make it a region rather than a `div` with
    `overflow-x: auto` on it:

      - `role="region"` with an `aria-label`, so a screen reader announces what is being scrolled rather
        than dropping the visitor into an unnamed box. `Label` is required for that reason.
      - `tabindex="0"`, because a region that only a mouse can scroll is content a keyboard cannot reach.
        Browsers do not make a scroll container focusable on their own.
      - The edge affordance in `marten-studio.css`, driven by the `data-scroll-start` / `data-scroll-end`
        attributes `martenStudio.tableScroll` writes. It appears only while there is something hidden on
        that side, so a table that fits shows nothing.

    The component itself calls no JavaScript. The scrolling is the browser's own `overflow-x: auto` and
    works with no script at all - the cue is the only part that needs one, and it is driven by a single
    document-wide observer in marten-studio.js rather than by one interop registration per table. That
    observer also adopts `.ms-table-wrap`, the documents list's own long-standing scroll container,
    which lives in a component this packet does not own; a per-component registration could never have
    reached it. Nothing here holds a `DotNetObjectReference`, so nothing here can leak one.

    The wrapper deliberately draws no border of its own: `.ms-table` already has one, with its own
    radius, and a second would read as a frame round a frame.
*@

<div class="ms-table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="@Label" tabindex="0">
    @ChildContent
</div>

@code {
    /// <summary>
    /// What this region is, for the accessibility tree - "Event types table", "Activity table".
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Required, and not defaulted to something generic: a page with four scrollable regions on it
    /// (Configuration has exactly that) announces four identical landmarks if they are all called
    /// "Table", which is worse than useless. <see cref="Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.EditorRequiredAttribute" />
    /// makes leaving it out a build error in this repository, where warnings are errors.
    /// </remarks>
    [Parameter]
    [EditorRequired]
    public string Label { get; set; } = string.Empty;

    /// <summary>The table.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public RenderFragment? ChildContent { get; set; }
}
